.Dd May 4, 2026
.Dt DIRHASH 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm dirhash
.Nd content-based directory tree hash
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl rte
.Ar path ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility computes a BLAKE3 content fingerprint for each
.Ar path
argument.
A path may be a regular file or a directory.
.Pp
For a directory,
.Nm
walks the tree, hashes every regular file with BLAKE3, and folds the
sorted multiset of child hashes into a Merkle-style hash for each
subdirectory \(em bottom up.
The result is name-agnostic: two directory trees with identical content
under different filenames hash to the same value.
Symlinks and other non-regular entries are excluded.
.Pp
By default,
.Nm
prints one line per
.Ar path
argument in
.Xr md5 1 Ns -compatible
form, with directories distinguished by a trailing slash:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<hex>  <path>
<hex>  <path>/
.Ed
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Fl
.It Fl r
Recursive: print a hash line for every node visited (post-order \(em
children before their parent), not just the
.Ar path
roots.
.It Fl t
Tagged output, in the BSD
.Xr md5 1
style:
.Dl BLAKE3 (path) = <hex>
.It Fl e
Print per-file I/O errors to standard error.
Without this flag, unreadable files are silently skipped.
.It Fl h
Show usage and exit.
.El
.Sh I/O BEHAVIOUR
Every file is opened with
.Dv POSIX_FADV_SEQUENTIAL
and closed with
.Dv POSIX_FADV_DONTNEED ,
which protects the ZFS ARC working set on
.Fx
and avoids polluting the page cache on Linux.
Files are read in 4 MiB chunks.
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
Non-zero status indicates that at least one
.Ar path
argument could not be hashed.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Hash a single backup snapshot:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dirhash /backup/2024-01
164ca99b...  /backup/2024-01/
.Ed
.Pp
Compare two snapshots node-by-node:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ dirhash -r /backup/2024-01 | sort > a.list
$ dirhash -r /backup/2024-02 | sort > b.list
$ diff a.list b.list
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr dir_hash 3 ,
.Xr md5 1 ,
.Xr fts 3 ,
.Xr posix_fadvise 2
.Sh AUTHORS
The
.Nm
utility and library were authored by
.An Keith Williams .
